# Textbook operations

This file is the machinery of the two tracks. Why the tracks exist and what each may claim is in `audience.mdc`; how a page is written is in `textbook-prose.mdc`. Neither is restated here.

## On-ramp page shape

Each `docs/on-ramp/NN-<slug>.mdx` is a full chapter page under every rule in `textbook-prose.mdc`, and additionally:

- **May assume** its parent chapter, every earlier chapter, and every earlier on-ramp page. **May not assume** a later chapter's terms. Where the last main chapter (currently Chapter 14, From futures to options) owns the word, describe the experience in plain words and forward-link Chapter 14 ("Chapter 14 calls this …").
- **Fixed content**, in the page's sections: the seat change for this chapter's object · the canonical toy retold from the customer seat · what the chapter's screen tells the holder about their own position and what it cannot · the one thing that changes for a holder and never changes for a futures trader · a Chapter 14 forward link.
- Terms it teaches get `TermFirst` and a glossary row with `firstDefinedIn: 'on-ramp/NN-slug'`.
- Closers do not retell the seat change or the one-thing-that-changes; they connect, case, or produce from them.

## Navigation

- `sidebars.ts`: each main chapter is a category whose `link` is the chapter doc and whose single item is its on-ramp page, so pagination runs chapter → on-ramp → next chapter. The `chapter(id, label, onRamp)` helper builds one.
- Each main chapter's Gate (or "After …" section) carries one line of this shape:
  `On-ramp: [title](/docs/on-ramp/NN-slug) — <one clause naming what the on-ramp retells from the customer seat>.`
  The prefix, link, and dash are fixed; the clause is the page's own and is not a thesis reprise.
- The last main chapter (currently Chapter 14, From futures to options) is the destination of the on-ramp track. Its own on-ramp page is the first week of its Stage 1, not a summary.

## Adding a main chapter

In one edit: the chapter page; its on-ramp page (or an explicit "on-ramp pending" note in the Gate); glossary rows for every term either page teaches; the `sidebars.ts` category; the Gate `On-ramp:` line. Then the checks in `textbook-prose.mdc`.

## Moving a term

A term the on-ramp track needs earlier than a main chapter teaches it is a **proposed move**, never a silent addition. State the term, from, to, and why, and wait for approval, because a move changes the glossary id's `firstDefinedIn`, every `TermFirst` placement, the forward-vocabulary order, and the last main chapter's structure. Ids never change (see glossary change control in `textbook-prose.mdc`).

## Adjacent blocks when restructuring

When a chapter is split or merged, each adjacent item follows the chapter whose object it bears on, and its `firstDefinedIn` is updated in the same edit. Re-run the coverage search for every item: a split can teach what an item used to park.
